# WellMate Engineering Memory

This file is the persistent engineering ledger for repository changes.

## Repository baseline
- Repository: sarkarshivaditya-lab/WellMate
- Default branch: main
- Primary stack: React 19, TypeScript 5.9, Vite 7, React Router 7, Auth0, Convex, Capacitor 7, Tailwind CSS.
- Browser authentication uses Auth0; native Capacitor authentication has a dedicated callback handler.
- Convex is the backend and authenticated user records are associated through tokenIdentifier.
- Reliability architecture is documented in RELIABILITY.md.
- CI currently runs lint, TypeScript checking, deterministic emergency tests, and production build.
- Onboarding state uses localStorage keys including onboarding_profile and onboarded.

## Engineering constraints
- Do not patch symptoms while leaving duplicate or conflicting architecture behind.
- Trace callers and dependencies before structural changes.
- Preserve offline-first and recovery guarantees.
- Never edit convex/_generated manually.
- Authentication changes require end-to-end flow analysis across browser and native paths.
- Every completed code edit must be recorded below.

## Non-negotiable deployment invariant

**Do not regress the currently deployed application.** Existing production behavior is the compatibility baseline. Future work must build on that baseline unless an explicit migration is part of the task.

Before every substantial edit:
- Establish the current behavior and affected dependency chain.
- Preserve existing routes, auth flow, persistence keys/data, Convex contracts, environment variables, sync/recovery semantics, build/deployment configuration, and native integration behavior unless explicitly changing them.
- Prefer additive/compatibility-first migrations.
- Validate both static correctness and affected runtime behavior.
- Record every code change and its compatibility impact in this file.

## Additional engineering controls

- Legacy code must be proven unused before removal.
- Persisted data changes require backwards-compatible reads or an explicit migration.
- Cross-layer edits require dependency and integration tracing.
- Substantial changes require release-style verification and a documented rollback/compatibility strategy.

